<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Armanum</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Armanum was an ancient city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> whose exact location is uncertain. Akkadian royal inscriptions record that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> conquered it together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a>. Its capture is cited as an example of the military reach of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Armanum is thought to have lain in the same general area as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a>, but where exactly is disputed. Many scholars identify it with Armi or Armium, a city often named in the archives of Ebla, and some take it to be the 3rd-millennium BCE name of Halpa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>; Tall Bazi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> has also been proposed. Alfonso Archi rejected the identification with Armi, and another reading holds that the Ebla Naram-Sin names was not the north Syrian city but another, Transtigridian Ebla. According to the archaeologist Matthiae, writing in 2020, after the fall of Mari and of Nagar, modern Tell Brak, Ebla and Armanum were the only significant centres left in the region, Armanum being the stronger.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a> named a year of his reign after the conquest of Armanum and the tearing down of its walls, and added the phrase conqueror of Armanum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a> to his titles; the words Smiter of Armanum and Ebla appear on a marble lamp and a slate plaque from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Telloh</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> bowl from Luristan. An Old Babylonian copy found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> of the inscription on one of his statues says that the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a> gave Naram-Sin Armanum and Ebla, together with the Amanus, the Cedar Mountain and the Upper Sea, and that he captured Rid-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a>, king of Armanum.</p><p>The same text describes Armanum on a high hill with three walls, giving the heights of hill and walls in cubits to a total of 404 cubits from the ground to the top of the wall, and says the king undermined the city. A fragment of an Akkadian inscription found by Iraqi archaeologists at Tulul al-Baqarat in 2009 adds detail on the campaign, including the capture of 80,508 prisoners. Much later, the literary composition Gula-AN and the Seventeen Kings against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> names a Madagina as king of the Land of Armanum and calls Ris-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a> king of Apisal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Armanum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Naram-Sin's campaigns carried the empire to its greatest extent: he claimed the conquest of <b>Armanum</b> and Ebla in Syria and a victory over the ruler of Magan across the Gulf, and suppressed a great revolt of Sumerian and northern cities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
